.TH ORBLAB 2
.SH NAME
orblab: run, parse, masses \- Danby N-body models as scripts
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "sys.m";
include "draw.m";
include "orblab.m";
orblab := load Orblab Orblab->PATH;

err := orblab->run(ctxt, name, spec);
(m, bodies, exprs, err) := orblab->parse(spec);
mass := orblab->masses(m, exprs);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Orblab
is the Danby orbital viewer \(em window, integrator, trails and controls
\(em as a library the model calls:
.PP
.EX
orblab->run(ctxt, "threebody", SPEC);
.EE
.PP
The vocabulary is
.IR dynsys (2)'s
\(em
.BR param ,
.BR maxtime ,
.BR state ,
.BR derivative ,
.BR eq ,
.B observable
\(em plus
.BR title ,
.B manual
and one line of orblab's own:
.TP
.BI body " name expr"
A body, its label on the diagram, and its mass.  Repeatable.  The mass
is an EXPRESSION over the parameters, not a number, because in this
family a slider usually IS a mass; it is evaluated at every reset.
.PP
The state is four values per body in declaration order \(em
.IR x ,
.IR y ,
then the two velocity components \(em so a spec with three bodies has
twelve
.B state
lines.
.I Orblab
checks the count rather than letting a mis-shaped spec draw nonsense.
Exactly two
.B observable
lines are required; they are the two traces.
.PP
.B Masses
evaluates the mass expressions
.B parse
returned.  The parameters must already be bound, which
.B dynsys->initialstate()
does, so call it straight after that.
.PP
The window is Tk's.  The parameter sliders are Tk scales, the buttons are
Tk buttons, the status line is a Tk label, and the layout is a Tk
geometry manager's; the orbit diagram and the graph are two
.IR panel (9)
widgets holding images the library draws into.  The widgets are
configured to the same night-sky colours as the diagram, so the window is
one picture rather than a dark rectangle in a bright frame.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/orblab.b
